  "nl_text": "This database schema, schemabench_0074, is used for a URL shortener service. It contains four tables: clicks, links, migrations, and users.\nThe clicks table stores information about each click on a shortened URL. Each row represents a single click, and the columns include the IP address of the user who clicked, the country they are from, the referer (the page they came from), the user agent (the browser they used), the ID of the link they clicked, the date and time of the click, and the updated date and time of the click. The link ID is a foreign key that references the ID column in the links table.\nThe links table stores information about each shortened URL. Each row represents a single URL, and the columns include the ID of the URL, the shortened URL, the long URL, the IP address of the user who created the URL, the ID of the user who created the URL, the number of clicks on the URL, the secret key used to protect the URL, whether the URL is disabled, whether the URL is custom, whether the URL is an API, the date and time the URL was created, the date and time the URL was updated, and the hash of the long URL. The creator ID is a foreign key that references the ID column in the users table.\nThe migrations table stores information about each database migration. Each row represents a single migration, and the columns include the migration name and the batch number.\nThe users table stores information about each user of the URL shortener service. Each row represents a single user, and the columns include the ID of the user, the username, the password, the email address, the IP address, the recovery key, the role of the user, whether the user is active, the API key, whether the API key is active, the API quota, the date and time the user was created, the date and time the user was updated, and the date and time the user was deleted (if applicable).\nIn summary, this schema is used to store information about shortened URLs, clicks on those URLs, users of the URL shortener service, and database migrations. It includes foreign keys to ensure referential integrity and to enforce entity integrity.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nIntrepid is a self-hostable, open-source URL shortener designed to provide users with the ability to brand their URLs and maintain control over their data. Based on the platform Polr, the application aims to offer a quick and modern approach to link shortening with a robust API.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective is to develop an efficient, self-hosted URL shortening solution that allows users to manage and brand their own links while providing data control and insights into link usage.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Primary Users: Individuals and organizations seeking a customizable and private link shortening service.\n- Administrators: Responsible for maintaining the application, managing user accounts, and overseeing system functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- User Registration & Management: \n  - Users can register and manage their accounts, including setting up passwords and recovery keys.\n  - Specific user roles and permissions are assigned for accessing various functionalities.\n\n- Link Creation & Management:\n  - Users can create shortened URLs from long URLs, with options for branding and customization.\n  - Option to generate custom URLs and API-based links.\n\n- Tracking & Analytics:\n  - The system logs clicks, including IP addresses, countries, referers, and user agents.\n  - Provides click analytics for each link, such as total clicks and click sources.\n\n- API Functionality:\n  - A robust API allows programmatic access to shortening features, including link creation and management.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: \n  - The application should perform efficiently under varying loads, supporting both individual and organizational use.\n\n- Security:\n  - Ensure secure processing of user data and protection against unauthorized API usage through keys and quotas.\n\n- Scalability:\n  - The system should support scaling to accommodate increasing numbers of users and links, while maintaining performance.\n\n- Availability:\n  - The application should be reliably available for use, with minimal downtime and timely updates to address user needs and security patches.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0074.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0074\nCREATE TABLE `clicks` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `referer` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_agent` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `link_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `clicks_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`link_id`) REFERENCES `links` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `links` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `shortened_url` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `long_url` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `creator_ip` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `creator_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `click_count` int(11) DEFAULT 0,\n  `secret_key` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_disabled` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `is_custom` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `is_api` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `long_url_hash` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `links_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`creator_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `migrations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `migration_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `batch_number` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `recovery_key` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  `api_key` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_api_key_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `api_quota` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `deleted_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
